##### 2. Domain-Driven Design (DDD)

**Strategic Patterns:**

| Pattern | Purpose | When to Use |
|---------|---------|-------------|
| **Bounded Context** | Define clear boundaries around domain models | Separate subdomains with different business concepts/rules |
| **Ubiquitous Language** | Shared vocabulary between business and technical teams | All code, documentation, and conversations use business terms |
| **Context Mapping** | Define relationships between bounded contexts | Multiple teams/services need to integrate different domains |
| **Domain Events** | Communicate state changes within/across domains | Decouple components, enable event-driven architecture |

**Tactical Patterns:**

| Pattern | Purpose | Implementation |
|---------|---------|----------------|
| **Entity** | Object with unique identity | Claims, Patients, Providers - tracked over time |
| **Value Object** | Immutable object defined by attributes | Address, Money, DateRange - no identity |
| **Aggregate** | Cluster of entities/value objects with consistency boundary | ClaimAggregate (Claim + ClaimLines + Adjustments) |
| **Repository** | Abstraction for data access | IClaimRepository, IPatientRepository |
| **Domain Service** | Business logic that doesn't fit in entities | ClaimAdjudicationService, EligibilityVerificationService |
| **Application Service** | Orchestrate domain operations for use cases | SubmitClaimHandler, ApproveClaimHandler |

**DDD Example - Bounded Context Structure:**

